{"qid":"Q1019","names":["Madagascar"],"title":"Madagascar","endonym":{"native":"République de Madagascar","lang":"fr"},"capsule":"The Republic of Madagascar gained independence from France on 26 June 1960 under President Philibert Tsiranana, succeeding a French colonial territory whose administration had followed the violent suppression of the 1947 revolt and the earlier conquest of the Merina kingdom in 1896. Post-independence history has been marked by repeated political ruptures: a 1972 military coup ended Tsiranana's rule, Didier Ratsiraka governed under a Marxist constitution from 1975, multiparty elections in 1993 brought Albert Zafy to power, and Andry Rajoelina came to power in a 2009 coup subsequently legitimized by the 2018 elections. Madagascar remains among the world's least developed countries despite exceptional biodiversity, with an economy anchored in rice agriculture, vanilla exports, and small-scale mining.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. As a sovereign island state, Madagascar's territory is precisely defined; no cartographic ambiguity exists for the period covered.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Madagascar","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Madagascar"},{"label":"Britannica: Madagascar","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Madagascar"}],"britannica":"place/madagascar","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Madagascar","rulers":[{"qid":"Q315944","name":"Philibert Tsiranana","start":1959,"end":1972,"image":"Philibert Tsiranana 1962.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Philibert%20Tsiranana%201962.jpg?width=320","imageLicense":{"license":"CC B